<title>FRANCE: EU's Brittan open to action on Korean cars.</title>
<headline>EU's Brittan open to action on Korean cars.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-10-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Trade Commissioner Sir Leon Brittan said on Thursday he was willing to take up the issue of Korean car exports to Europe but European carmakers would have to provide evidence of any unfair competition.</p>
<p>&quot;We have a deal with Japan (on exports to the EU), but on Korean cars we need to see if there is a question of unfair competition or dumping. I'm waiting for European industry to provide evidence,&quot; Brittan told a European forum here.</p>
<p>If such evidence was provided, he said he would be willing to &quot;approach the international institutions to address the issues&quot;.</p>
<p>The European carmakers association ACEA said in early October it had set up a working group to examine the inroads which South Korean carmakers were making into their market.</p>
<p>The Koreans' share of the European car market has jumped to 1.8 percent in August from 0.2 percent in 1990. ACEA officials say European carmakers have only boosted their share of South Korea's market to 0.4 percent from 0.1 percent over the same period.</p>
</text>
